LEAD = {
    "headline": "SCRIPTED SITCOMS ON INSTAGRAM WENT FROM 44 EPISODES LAST OCTOBER TO 136 THIS OCTOBER, BRANDS ARE PAYING TEN TO A HUNDRED THOUSAND DOLLARS AN EPISODE FOR THEM, AND THE THING THAT MAKES THEM WORK IS THAT NO EPISODE NEEDS YOU TO HAVE SEEN THE LAST ONE",
    "deck": "Business Insider published the first real count of this format this morning, and the count is the story.  Episode supply tripled in twelve months.  Brand money is already inside it, at ten thousand to a hundred thousand dollars a show.  And every producer quoted gives the same reason it works, which is the exact opposite of how every brand series you have ever been pitched was sold to you.  These shows are built so that nobody ever needs to have watched the previous one.",
    "stamps": [
        ("BUSINESS INSIDER · 6 NOV", "https://www.businessinsider.com/golden-age-of-instagram-social-sitcoms-video-has-arrived-2026-11"),
        ("LINK IN BIO · BILT ROOMIES", "https://www.linkinbio.news/p/inside-bilts-popular-roomies-series"),
    ],
    "body": [
        "The count first.  Jon Stahl is a television producer who worked on Veep and now tracks this format.  His analysis of <mark>34 scripted short-form shows on Instagram</mark> puts them at <mark>over 1.4 billion views since 2023</mark>.  And the supply line is the number that matters: he counted <mark>44 short-form sitcom episodes released in October 2025, and 136 in October 2026</mark>.  Tripled in a year.  He is not a neutral observer, which we come back to below.",
        "What these shows are, concretely.  Secondhand Exchange has done <mark>24 episodes in under five months</mark> and holds <mark>130,000 followers and 17 million views</mark>, at three minutes an episode.  Near Death, which Stahl makes and which stars the Veep actor Matt Walsh, put out its first episode in August and did <mark>10 million views across Instagram and Facebook in about a month</mark>.  Brooklyn Coffee Shop has <mark>691,000 followers</mark>, and its biggest episode, a crossover with another show called Mechanical Pencil, did <mark>over 10 million views, 13,000 comments and 267,000 shares</mark>.  The Ick runs at roughly a minute an episode and has 700,000 followers.  A fourth show, Flirting!, launched in August and has 94,000.",
        "Now the mechanism, and three different people in the piece describe it the same way without using the same word.  Same set, same cast, a new guest every episode, so <mark>every episode is a valid first episode</mark>.  NowThis editor in chief Michael Vito Valentino: every episode can be someone's first, and that shapes everything from the writing to the pacing.  Mutuals Media chief executive Amelia Montooth says recognisability is what gets people <mark>past the three-second mark, where you lose half your audience</mark>.  Stahl calls it repeatability.  The structure exists because the viewer arrives by accident, mid-scroll, having seen nothing.",
        "The money is already in there and it is not large.  Several industry insiders told Business Insider that brand deals for short-form scripted shows <mark>ran from 10,000 to 100,000 dollars</mark>, with bigger shows and more deliverables pushing into six figures.  Tory Burch has bought an episode of Brooklyn Coffee Shop, and Valentino has bought one of Flirting!  The fullest brand case is older: Bilt, a rent-payment company, funded a series called Roomies about roommates, and by August 2025 eight episodes had done <mark>over 8 million views and 115,000 followers</mark> across TikTok and Instagram, with Bilt's Cyrus Ferguson later saying each episode can generate half a million views and declining to say what it costs.  At the other end, the producers of a show called Friend Material told The Ankler in July that it was made for <mark>1,000 dollars</mark> and had been viewed nearly 1.5 million times in under three months.",
        "Two honest problems with all of it.  First, Stahl produces these shows — five scripted social series since February 2025, two of which he says went nowhere — so the person counting the format's growth sells the format.  Second, the platform pays almost nothing for it.  Jonah Feingold, who makes Flirting!, on what YouTube returns: I can buy somebody lunch.  Instagram has no revenue share on this at all.  Which means brand money is not one funding option among several here.  It is the only money in the room, and that is why the format is being built to be cheap.",
    ],
    "numbers": [
        ("136", "scripted short-form sitcom episodes went out on Instagram in October 2026, against 44 in October 2025, on one producer's own count"),
        ("1.4B", "views across the 34 scripted Instagram shows he tracks, accumulated since 2023"),
        ("$10K", "the bottom of the brand-deal range insiders described for one of these shows, with the top of the range at 100,000 dollars"),
    ],
    "flagnote": "Every supply and view figure here comes from Jon Stahl's own private tracking, relayed by Business Insider, which publishes no methodology, no snapshot date and no indication that it checked his figures; Stahl produces shows in this format, so the growth he counts is also his own market.  The article's follower table carries no source line and no date at all, and it contradicts the article's own prose on Secondhand Exchange, giving 132,000 followers where the text says 130,000.  The per-show view counts are platform-reported Instagram and Facebook figures relayed by creators or by Stahl; only the Brooklyn Coffee Shop crossover figures are checkable on the post itself.  The 10,000 to 100,000 dollar brand-deal range is attributed to several unnamed industry insiders with no deal documents and no sample.  The three-second retention figure is Montooth's assertion in an interview with no data or study behind it.  No production budget has been disclosed for any brand-funded show in this format; Bilt explicitly declined.  The Bilt figures are from August and December 2025 and are carried here as context rather than as current numbers, and Business Insider's own table now puts Roomies at 200,000 Instagram followers.  The 1,000 dollar Friend Material figure is a production cost reported in July, not a brand fee.  Nobody has published what Instagram or Meta pays for a scripted series, so that is unknown rather than zero.",
    "so_what": "Every brand series anybody has ever sold you was sold on continuity.  Episode one builds the world, episode four pays it off, the audience accumulates, and by the end you own something.  This format is built on the assumption that the audience never accumulates at all — it arrives one person at a time, at a random episode, having missed everything before it.  So the production design is about removing the cost of entry rather than rewarding loyalty: one set you can shoot in a day, a cast you can book repeatedly, and a premise explained by the first shot.  That is also why the numbers are achievable on ten thousand dollars.  The expensive parts of a brand series are the parts that only pay off if somebody watches in order, and almost nobody does.",
    "do_this": "Take the brand series or branded show you have in development and ask one question of every episode: could somebody who has seen nothing else understand this in the first five seconds.  Where the answer is no, find out what is carrying the context, because that is usually a recap, a title card or a cold open written to catch people up, and all three are the parts you can cut.  Then change what you ask for in a pitch: request the share count per episode for the last six episodes rather than total views, because shares are the signal the people making these shows actually steer on and they are the figure a brand can publish without disclosing spend.  And if you want to test the format, buy a single episode of an existing show for ten thousand dollars before you commission a series, because that price now exists and did not a year ago.",
}

